grund cover — group the citation graph by scanned file.

Usage:  grund cover [PATH] [--glob PATTERN]... [--format text|json]
        grund cover FILE --lines N|N-M [--lines …] [--format text|json]

With no PATH, the run covers the whole project or workspace discovered from the current directory; a PATH narrows it.
The command runs the same scan as `check` and `refs`,
then prints one file record with the citations found in that file.
With --glob a record keeps only the citations whose cited coordinate a pattern
matches, and every scanned file keeps its record: `(no citations)` in text.
With --lines it prints instead, for each range of FILE, the declaration
and section that own its lines.

Options:
  --format text|json   text (default) groups citations by file; json emits one record per file.
  --glob PATTERN       keep citations of what it matches; repeatable; not with --lines.
                       e.g. grund cover src/ --glob 'FS-login.**'
  --lines N|N-M        report the owners of these lines of FILE; repeatable, one record per range.
  --path-base project|invocation
                       project (default) spells report paths from the config root;
                       invocation: relative to the path you passed, or the current directory if you passed none.
                       e.g. grund cover src/ --path-base=invocation
                       --path-base=invocation replaces a committed `[output] relative_paths = false`.
                       --format json replaces a committed `[output] format = "json"`; a flag outranks its key.

Exit:  0 scan succeeded · 2 unreadable tree, incomplete scan, a refused --glob, or CLI error.

Examples:
  grund cover src/                # source files and their spec citations
  grund cover --format json       # machine-readable coverage index
  grund cover src/ --glob 'FS-login{,.**}' # where each file cites FS-login
  grund cover docs/FS-x.md --lines 105-121   # which spec points a diff hunk edits
